# Raw loops in the search's hot paths are deliberate. The suggested algorithms
# express the same work, but these loops carry early exits and side effects that
# the algorithm forms obscure, and this code is measured, not guessed at
# (architecture 8k measured where node time actually goes).
useStlAlgorithm

# `for (const auto& d : delta)` over a C array of int[2]. The suggested pointer
# form is not clearer, and the reference form is already const.
# Deliberately not pinned to a line number. It was pinned to board.h:35, and
# adding the per-file licence notice moved the code to line 50 and silently
# stopped the suppression matching, failing CI for a finding that had already
# been declined. A file-scoped suppression survives edits above it.
constVariableReference:src/board.h

# `move_to_front(std::vector<Move>&, const Move&)` genuinely mutates its
# argument: on a hit it calls std::rotate(begin, begin+i, begin+i+1), which
# makes element i the new first. The analyser does not model std::rotate's
# write through the range, so it concludes the parameter is only read. Making
# it const would not compile, and if it somehow did the proof-hint ordering
# would silently become a no-op.
# (An empty "#" line here is not a comment to every cppcheck version: older
# parsers strip the # and read an empty suppression id, failing the whole file.)
# Only reported by the CI runner's cppcheck, not by 2.18.0 locally, which is
# why it appeared on the first run rather than during development.
constParameterReference:src/ordering.h

# `thpn > total.pn ? sat_add(chosen.pn, thpn - total.pn) : chosen.pn` in the DFPN
# threshold update. The analyser proves the condition always true, because the
# loop guard above returns whenever `total.pn >= thpn`, and it is right about
# that today. The ternary is not redundancy, it is a guard against UNSIGNED
# UNDERFLOW: these are uint32, and `thpn - total.pn` with the inequality the
# other way is not a small negative number, it is about four billion, which
# saturating addition would then turn into an effectively infinite threshold and
# a subtree that never terminates. Deleting the branch would be correct only for
# as long as the guard above it stays exactly as it is.
knownConditionTrueFalse:src/dfpn.h

# `print_cert_tree(std::ostream&, CertReader&, Board b, int, bool)` takes the
# board BY VALUE on purpose, and so does print_cert_node below it. A defender
# branch mutates the position as it walks its line, and every sibling branch has
# to start from the same board -- the copy is what makes the tree a tree rather
# than one long line. A const reference would not compile against the callee,
# and a mutable reference would give each branch the previous branch's leftovers.
passedByValue:src/report.h
